<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">K'inich Yat Ahk II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 781–808</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Ruler 7</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">K'inich Yat Ahk II was the last known king of the Classic Maya city of Piedras Negras on the Usumacinta river, ruling from 781. His reign produced fine monuments, including a carved throne. In 808 he was captured by the rival kingdom of Yaxchilan, and Piedras Negras declined rapidly afterwards and was abandoned in the early 9th century.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>K'inich Yat Ahk II, known to scholars as Ruler 7, was the son of Lady Bird and was born on 7 April 750. His father may have been Itzam Kʼan Ahk II, or perhaps Tʼul Chiik, a prince of Piedras Negras. His name joins a title and a predecessor's name: kʼinich, meaning red-faced, referred to the belief of the city's rulers that they were lords of the sun, while Yat Ahk was taken not from a recent ruler but from one of the earliest, Yat Ahk I.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>He took the throne on 31 May 781, almost a year after the death of his brother Haʼ Kʼin Xook, the sixth ruler, and no one is known to have ruled in between. In August 787 he captured a yajaw kʼahk, a lord of fire, from Santa Elena Poco Uinic. In 792 and 794 he fought two star wars, timed to coincide with astronomical events, against the rival city of Pomona with his ally Parrot Chaak of La Mar; the second left Pomona decisively defeated and subordinated.</p><p>His monuments include Stela 15, which marked his first hotun as ruler and shows him almost in three dimensions, and Stela 12, a shallow relief of the victory over Pomona with named captives, which Stephen D. Houston and colleagues read as a monument of vengeance for the defeat of Piedras Negras by Pomona in 554. He probably commissioned Throne 1, which records the death or abdication of Haʼ Kʼin Xook, and Panel 3, which recalls a kʼatun celebration held in 749 by Itzam Kʼan Ahk II and is regarded by many Mayanists as a masterpiece of Maya art.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 808 Yaxchilan, ruled by Kʼinich Tatbu Skull IV, conquered Piedras Negras, and Lintel 10 at Yaxchilan names K'inich Yat Ahk II as a captive of its ruler. Debris and burnt objects across the site, and the deliberately dismantled Throne 1, suggest that troops from Yaxchilan razed the city. Researchers of the 1930s proposed a peasants' revolt instead, an idea modern scholars largely reject. Simon Martin, Nikolai Grube and Stephen D. Houston argue that the dynasty may have survived his capture and death for a time, with much reduced power, until around 810.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kʼinich Yat Ahk II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 808 Yaxchilan captured <b>K'inich Yat Ahk II</b>, the last known king, and the city declined swiftly, being abandoned by the early 9th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/piedras-negras">Piedras Negras</a></li></ul></section>
